<p>A Los Angeles jury found Meta and Google’s YouTube liable for harming a 20-year-old woman who said she became compulsively attached to the platforms at a young age. Jurors concluded that Instagram and YouTube were negligently designed or operated and that the companies failed to warn users about the risks. They awarded $6 million in total damages: $3 million in compensatory damages and $3 million in punitive damages. Meta was assigned 70% of the compensatory share and Google 30%. The case is widely seen as a landmark test of whether social media companies can be held responsible for alleged addiction-related mental health harms. Several outlets said the verdict could shape more than a thousand similar pending cases in the US and encourage closer scrutiny in other countries. Both companies said they disagreed with the verdict. Google said it plans to appeal, while Meta said it is evaluating its legal options. One notable feature of the trial was the plaintiffs’ focus on platform design rather than specific user-generated content. Several reports said that strategy made it harder for the companies to rely on their usual legal defenses.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/zALAtc3S7-GaLeH0SBEN52Rt8IFZj-r2m1k-X2wBBafK3DUWRFEbbSFyJlWWl-YBfFqrjEWacsZjMPUzCpDJj5s2eR7J0msPW3WS2JIbbimD_1JU9gNALMM_fE3Yc7oyb1Wsdk4na4AiBOoy39vnnY7KeTLlNE15vsNiFnQ2MITeQBwJIYQQ04PwQ-3R525O4UxpFtGwv_x47SqIxE0t0vBfY-26Vn1A1MVrStlPpwiPs3r2HmQoR9fFfoK0LIuVACp-ZwJYncY5k7flMA' alt='Lori Schott, mother of Annalee, is embraced by lawyer Laura Marquez-Garrett outside court after the jury found Meta and Google liable in Los Angeles on March 25, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Long deliberations: Jurors deliberated for roughly 43 hours over nine days before reaching the decision, underscoring how closely contested the evidence and legal questions were.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Plaintiff lawyers: The legal team described the result as an industry-wide accountability moment and said the case showed juries are willing to scrutinize platform product choices. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/meta-google-liable-for-social-media-addiction-us-jury/'>Michael West Media</a>)</li><li>Legal analysts: Semafor said the verdict strengthens the argument that social media platforms can cause personal injury, potentially influencing other active cases. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/03/25/2026/youtube-meta-lose-landmark-social-media-addiction-case'>Semafor</a>)</li><li>Australian law firms: Lawyers in Australia said the US outcome suggests courts there may also be open to claims that social media platforms caused real-world harm, and some firms are now exploring the scope for local cases. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/law/2026/mar/26/tech-companies-social-media-addictive-products-meta-youtube'>The Guardian</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3347912/jury-finds-meta-and-google-liable-us3-million-damages-social-media-addiction-trial'>Meta and Google liable for damages in landmark US addiction trial</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/meta-google-liable-for-social-media-addiction-us-jury/'>Meta, Google liable for social media addiction: US jury</a> - michaelwest.com.au</li><li><a href='https://www.csmonitor.com/USA/Justice/2026/0325/verdicts-social-media-meta-instagram-youtube'>Juries find social media platforms are harming teens’ health</a> - csmonitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/d3d80bd4-d2b1-4522-9752-4071df5b4c0a'>Meta and Google liable for harm to children’s mental health in landmark US case</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c747x7gz249o'>Meta and YouTube found liable in landmark social media addiction trial</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/law/2026/mar/26/tech-companies-social-media-addictive-products-meta-youtube'>Law firms investigate possible Australian cases